{
  "text": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its induced norm and with the Borel σ-algebra of its norm topology. Write d = finrank_ℝ(E) ∈ ℕ, let γ_E be the centered standard Gaussian probability measure with identity covariance, and let λ_E be the canonical Lebesgue measure normalized by orthonormal coordinates. For every probability measure μ on E and every real η > 0, define Φ_η : E × E → E × E by Φ_η(x,z) = (x, x + √η · z). Then the following equality of measures on E × E holds:\n\n(Φ_η)_*(μ ⊗ γ_E) = (μ ⊗ λ_E).withDensity((x,y) ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d · exp(−‖y−x‖²/(2η)))).\n\nThe reference measure on the right is μ in the first coordinate and canonical Lebesgue measure λ_E in the second coordinate; it is not Lebesgue measure in both coordinates. The input μ is arbitrary apart from being a Borel probability measure: no density, moment, or smoothness hypothesis on μ is imposed. The left side is the pushforward of the product probability measure, so it describes the joint law of (X, X + √η · Z) when X has law μ and Z has law γ_E independently. For every Borel set A ⊆ E × E, the right side assigns to A the nonnegative integral of the displayed extended-nonnegative density over A with respect to μ ⊗ λ_E.\n\nThe dimension d is a natural number and zero dimension is allowed; canonical volume in that case is unit mass on the one-point space. The power in the prefactor is ordinary natural-number exponentiation. All scalar operations are real, √ is the nonnegative real square root, π is the circle constant, and the norm is the inner-product norm. ENNReal.ofReal embeds nonnegative real numbers into extended nonnegative reals and sends negative real numbers to zero. Real division and reciprocal are totalized at zero, with 0⁻¹ = 0, but η > 0 makes the displayed denominators positive, so these expressions use their ordinary nonzero-denominator values. No arbitrary choice of a measure-space volume, separate completeness hypothesis, or nontriviality hypothesis is present.",
